1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,679
Episode Seventy-Five covered Spring Data, transactions, and N-plus-one pitfalls.

2
00:00:06,679 --> 00:00:13,275
Persistence without security is an open door — every API needs an access story.

3
00:00:13,275 --> 00:00:20,121
Spring Security is the standard filter chain for authentication and authorization.

4
00:00:20,121 --> 00:00:25,632
Modern apps mix sessions, JWTs, OAuth two, and method-level rules.

5
00:00:25,632 --> 00:00:31,226
Interviews expect you to separate who you are from what you may do.

6
00:00:31,226 --> 00:00:37,738
Today — authn versus authz, the filter chain, securing REST, and common traps.

7
00:00:37,738 --> 00:00:40,424
Episode Seventy-Six.

8
00:00:40,424 --> 00:00:42,572
Spring Security.

9
00:00:42,572 --> 00:00:48,564
Authentication answers who you are — authorization answers what you may do.

10
00:00:48,564 --> 00:00:54,156
Credentials become a SecurityContext with an Authentication principal.

11
00:00:54,156 --> 00:01:00,627
Roles and authorities express permissions — ROLE_USER is a convention, not magic.

12
00:01:00,627 --> 00:01:04,462
Fail closed — deny by default, grant explicitly.

13
00:01:04,462 --> 00:01:09,975
Never conflate logged-in with allowed — that is the classic auth bug.

14
00:01:09,975 --> 00:01:16,446
Say authn and authz separately in interviews — interviewers listen for the split.

15
00:01:16,446 --> 00:01:21,981
Spring Security is a filter chain in front of your servlet stack.

16
00:01:21,981 --> 00:01:28,538
SecurityFilterChain beans declare which paths need auth and which are public.

17
00:01:28,538 --> 00:01:36,202
UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter, BearerTokenAuthenticationFilter — specialized links.

18
00:01:36,202 --> 00:01:42,674
Once authenticated, AuthorizationFilter enforces request matchers and rules.

19
00:01:42,674 --> 00:01:49,146
Order matters — mis-ordered filters create confusing allow or deny behavior.

20
00:01:49,146 --> 00:01:55,192
Debug with spring-security DEBUG logs when a path is unexpectedly open.

21
00:01:55,192 --> 00:01:58,064
Securing REST APIs in practice.

22
00:01:58,064 --> 00:02:04,734
Stateless JWT or opaque tokens for services — sessions for browser apps.

23
00:02:04,734 --> 00:02:12,331
CSRF protection matters for cookie sessions — often disabled for pure bearer APIs.

24
00:02:12,331 --> 00:02:18,353
CORS is not security — it only relaxes browser same-origin rules.

25
00:02:18,353 --> 00:02:23,726
Use HTTPS everywhere — tokens on the wire are credentials.

26
00:02:23,726 --> 00:02:30,026
Resource servers validate tokens — authorization servers issue them.

27
00:02:30,026 --> 00:02:33,351
Method security complements URL rules.

28
00:02:33,351 --> 00:02:38,600
EnableMethodSecurity unlocks PreAuthorize and PostAuthorize.

29
00:02:38,600 --> 00:02:45,249
Express domain rules — hasRole, hasAuthority, or custom PermissionEvaluator.

30
00:02:45,249 --> 00:02:51,461
URL rules catch coarse paths — method rules protect service operations.

31
00:02:51,461 --> 00:02:57,673
SpEL in annotations is powerful — keep expressions readable and tested.

32
00:02:57,673 --> 00:03:03,972
Defense in depth — never rely on the UI alone to hide forbidden actions.

33
00:03:03,972 --> 00:03:07,471
Security practices that survive production.

34
00:03:07,471 --> 00:03:12,192
Least privilege — grant the smallest authority that works.

35
00:03:12,192 --> 00:03:17,237
Rotate secrets and signing keys — store them outside the repo.

36
00:03:17,237 --> 00:03:23,992
Log auth failures without leaking whether a username exists when policy forbids it.

37
00:03:23,992 --> 00:03:28,712
Test both positive and negative authorization paths in CI.

38
00:03:28,712 --> 00:03:34,246
Threat-model new endpoints — every PostMapping is an attack surface.

39
00:03:34,246 --> 00:03:36,160
Three common mistakes.

40
00:03:36,160 --> 00:03:41,902
One — permitAll on a broad ant pattern that swallows admin routes.

41
00:03:41,902 --> 00:03:47,469
Two — trusting client-sent roles inside an unsigned JWT payload.

42
00:03:47,469 --> 00:03:53,559
Three — disabling CSRF for cookie apps without understanding the risk.

43
00:03:53,559 --> 00:03:59,996
Also — putting authorization only in the frontend — backends must enforce.

44
00:03:59,996 --> 00:04:05,216
Security is a server responsibility — clients are untrusted.

45
00:04:05,216 --> 00:04:10,252
Interview question — how do you secure a Spring Boot REST API?

46
00:04:10,252 --> 00:04:16,019
Declare a SecurityFilterChain — authenticate requests, authorize paths.

47
00:04:16,019 --> 00:04:22,273
Prefer bearer tokens for stateless services — validate signatures and expiry.

48
00:04:22,273 --> 00:04:26,984
Map authorities to endpoints and critical service methods.

49
00:04:26,984 --> 00:04:32,507
Keep secrets external — HTTPS in transit, least privilege in design.

50
00:04:32,507 --> 00:04:38,274
Prove it with tests that assert four-oh-one and four-oh-three outcomes.

51
00:04:38,274 --> 00:04:42,811
Security holds the line — next we prove the system.

52
00:04:42,811 --> 00:04:46,280
Episode Seventy-Seven — Spring Testing.

53
00:04:46,280 --> 00:04:52,063
Unit, slice, and full-context tests that keep Spring apps honest.

54
00:04:52,063 --> 00:04:53,308
See you there.
